---
import ToolLayout from "@site/layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { referencesFor } from "@site/lib/references.ts";
import references from "../references.json";
import Caveat from "@site/components/Caveat.astro";
import { EXERCISES, EQUIPMENT, MUSCLES, isAssumed, isSupported, type Exercise, type Muscle } from "../web/catalogue.ts";
const { cite } = referencesFor(references as Parameters<typeof referencesFor>[0]);
const muscle = Object.fromEntries(MUSCLES.map((m) => [m.id, m.label]));
const equip = Object.fromEntries(EQUIPMENT.map((e) => [e.id, e.label]));
const needs = (n: string[][]) => n.length ? n.map((g) => g.map((e) => equip[e]).join(" or ")).join("; plus ") : "nothing";
const strength = EXERCISES.filter((e) => e.direct.length);
const cell = (e: Exercise, ms: Muscle[]) => ms.map((m) => muscle[m] + (isAssumed(e, m) ? " ◇" : isSupported(e, m) ? " †" : "")).join(", ") || "–";
const sourced = (e: Exercise) => Object.entries(e.sources ?? {}).map(([m, n]) => `${muscle[m]}: "${n}"`).join("; ");
const aerobic = EXERCISES.filter((e) => e.met?.length);
const band = (met: number) => (met >= 6 ? "vigorous" : met >= 3 ? "moderate" : "light");
import { PRESETS } from "../web/catalogue.ts";
const presetText = PRESETS.map((p) => `${p.label} (${p.equipment.map((e) => equip[e]).join(", ")})`).join("; ");
---
<ToolLayout slug="exercise-planner" page="methods">
  <h1>Methods and sources</h1>
  <p class="lede">How the exercise planner works, where its numbers come from, what has been checked, and what it can't tell you.</p>

  <h2>Inputs and defaults</h2>
  <p>Every setting is kept in the page address, so a link reproduces the result.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Range and notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Targets</td><td>None</td><td>Any of the {MUSCLES.length} muscle groups, and heart and lungs. "Major muscle groups (WHO)" chooses all muscle groups (see "Heart and lungs"). Nothing is chosen when the page opens, so the visitor picks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Equipment</td><td>None</td><td>Any of the {EQUIPMENT.length - 1} kinds of equipment, or a preset: {presetText}.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Include bodyweight exercises</td><td>On</td><td>A separate option, so a preset such as "Machines only" doesn't silently remove push-ups and squats.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Most exercises in a suggestion</td><td>10</td><td>1 to 10.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Number of suggestions</td><td>3</td><td>1 to 6.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How different the suggestions are</td><td>Some shared</td><td>All different (no exercise shared), Some shared (up to half), or Any.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Covered level</td><td>1 set</td><td>½, 1, 2 or 3 sets for each target muscle (1 set: one direct exercise, or two indirect ones).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exercises left out</td><td>None</td><td>Any exercise can be left out of every suggestion.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>How suggestions are found</h2>
  <p>
    You choose targets (any of {MUSCLES.length} muscle groups, and heart and lungs) and the equipment you have. The planner keeps
    the exercises your equipment allows and that help at least one target, then tries every combination of up to the
    number of exercises you allow. It ranks them by:
  </p>
  <ol class="prose">
    <li>how many targets are covered (a target muscle is covered when one set of each exercise in the list gives it
      at least 1 set, counted as below; heart and lungs is covered by any aerobic activity);</li>
    <li>then the fewest exercises;</li>
    <li>then the most work for the target muscles, up to twice the covered level;</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Exercises that do exactly the same for your targets (for example the bench press and the chest press machine, if
    your only targets are chest and triceps) are searched as one, then swapped for each other to make varied lists.
    The planner picks the best few lists that differ enough from each other ("All different" shares no exercises; "Some
    shared" lets a list share up to half its exercises with an earlier one), preferring, among equals, the more intense
    aerobic activity and the exercises that work more muscles overall. If there aren't enough lists that differ that
    much, the limit is loosened a step at a time, only lists as good as the best are used, and the page says how much
    the later suggestions share. A "covered" level above one set (2 or 3) is reached by listing different exercises
    for the same muscle, never the same exercise twice. If more than 30 exercises could help, it keeps
    the best three for each target plus those that help most overall, and the page says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The score.</strong> For each target muscle, the sets from one set of each exercise (1 per direct, ½ per
    indirect) are added up and capped at the covered level; heart and lungs counts as the covered level when any aerobic
    activity is in the list. The sum is the score, so 3 targets at a covered level of 1 give at most 3. An exercise is
    only added if it raises the score.
    Ties are broken by the fewest exercises, then by the sets on the targets capped at twice the covered level. Within
    lists of equal rank, the one with the highest-MET aerobic activity comes first (100 points per MET), then the one
    working the most sets on all muscles. The search stops after 400,000 steps and, if it does, the page says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Example.</strong> Targets chest and triceps, covered level 1: the bench press gives chest 1 and triceps ½
    (score 1.5 of 2); adding a triceps pushdown gives triceps 1½, capped at 1 (score 2 of 2: both covered).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Names and order.</strong> Where an exercise can be done with different equipment (a lateral raise with a dumbbell
    or a cable, a curl with a dumbbell, barbell, band or cable), the list shows the version for the equipment you ticked, and
    the general name where the version doesn't matter. Every version is counted the same: the muscles come from the movement,
    not the equipment. Where the study behind a classification used one version only (the Pelland et al. table names the
    dumbbell shoulder press and the overhead dumbbell extension, for example), the others are marked as assumed to load the same muscles. Within a suggestion, exercises are
    listed by how many muscle groups they work (most first), then holds and carries (which the studies behind the counting
    did not analyse), then heart-and-lungs activities. This order is for reading only and doesn't change what is suggested.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>How-to links.</strong> Where the American Council on Exercise (ACE) free exercise library has a page for an
    exercise, the suggestion links to it ("How to do it") for technique: starting position, movement and common faults. Where
    it doesn't, the link goes to the National Academy of Sports Medicine (NASM) exercise library (bench dip, pike push-up,
    burpee) or to the National Strength and Conditioning Association's excerpt from its exercise technique manual (face
    pull). Nothing the planner shows is taken from those pages, so they are not in the reference list and no copy is
    archived. Some pages show another version of the same movement (a barbell instead of dumbbells, standing instead of
    kneeling); the source and page title are shown so you can tell. The inverted row, band pull-apart, 45° back extension
    and hanging knee raise have no link yet: no page from these or similar organisations gave step-by-step instructions for them.
  </p>

  <h2>Counting sets: direct and indirect</h2>
  <p>
    A set of an exercise counts as <strong>1 set</strong> for a muscle it trains <em>directly</em> and as
    <strong>½ set</strong> for a muscle it trains <em>indirectly</em>. This is the "fractional" method of Pelland and
    colleagues, whose meta-regressions of resistance-training studies classified each set as direct (the measured muscle
    was likely the primary force generator) or indirect (the muscle was likely meaningfully trained but as a helper, or
    synergist), and found that the relative evidence for this "fractional" counting was the strongest of the methods they
    compared, for both muscle growth and strength <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />.
  </p>
  <p>
    An earlier review by Schoenfeld and colleagues found that multi-joint exercises such as presses, rows and squats
    can't be assumed to train every muscle involved as much as an exercise aimed at that muscle; for example, during
    squats and the leg press the hamstrings are much less active than the quadriceps. At the time, with little direct
    evidence, they advised counting sets one-for-one and adjusting by judgement <Fragment set:html={cite("schoenfeld-2019")} />.
    The planner follows the later, larger analysis and counts half.
  </p>

  <h2 id="which-muscles">Which muscles each exercise works</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>From the study.</strong> Pelland and colleagues list how they classified the exercises in the studies they
    analysed, for these muscles only: the quadriceps, the rectus femoris (one of the four quadriceps muscles), the
    hamstrings, the chest, the front of the shoulders (anterior deltoid), the trapezius, the triceps and the biceps
    (Table 1 of the published article; Table 1A of the archived preprint) <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />.
    Where that table classifies an exercise for one of those muscles, the planner follows it exactly. For example, the
    bench press trains the chest directly and the front shoulders and triceps indirectly; the lat pulldown and seated row
    train the biceps and trapezius indirectly; squats, lunges and the leg press don't count for the hamstrings. The
    authors note that their classification "was not wholly objective".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The planner's own classification.</strong> Every other assignment is the planner's own, made from anatomy.
    That includes all of the lats, glutes, lower back, abs, calves and forearms, which the table doesn't cover, and the
    covered muscles in exercises it doesn't list. Each exercise is described by the joint movements it loads (for
    example, knee and hip extension in a squat), and the muscles that produce those movements come from an open anatomy
    textbook <Fragment set:html={cite("openstax-ap2e")} />: for example the gluteus maximus extends the hip, the hamstrings
    bend the knee and extend the hip, the erector spinae is the main extensor of the spine, the latissimus dorsi pulls the
    upper arm down and back, and the gastrocnemius and soleus point the foot. A muscle producing the exercise's main
    movement counts as direct; one producing a secondary movement counts as indirect.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shoulders in three parts.</strong> The deltoid is split into front, side and rear, because its parts do
    different jobs: the front part flexes and turns the arm inwards, the side part raises it out to the side, and the rear
    part extends it and turns it outwards <Fragment set:html={cite("stokey-2024")} />. One "shoulders" group would
    call the shoulders covered by a face pull alone, which mainly works the rear. Only the front part is in Pelland and
    colleagues' table. Neither anatomy source says which muscles move the arm backwards at shoulder height (horizontal
    abduction, as in a face pull or band pull-apart); counting the rear deltoid there is the planner's own reading.
  </p>
  <Caveat kind="assumption">These assignments are marked ◇ in the planner and in the table below. They are a
    judgement made openly, not a measurement, and the counting method itself was only tested on the muscles and the kind
    of exercises in the study. Where studies that measured muscle growth support one, it is marked † instead
    (<a href="#growth-evidence">see below</a>).</Caveat>
  <h3 id="growth-evidence">Evidence from studies that measured muscle growth</h3>
  <p>
    The planner's own classifications are being compared, one muscle group at a time, with training studies that measured
    muscle size (by MRI or ultrasound) before and after, which say more than studies of muscle activity (EMG) alone. Where
    such studies show that a muscle grows with an exercise, it is marked † instead of ◇. That supports counting the muscle;
    whether it counts as a full or half set remains the planner's judgement.
  </p>
  <p>
    A study counts here if it trained people for several weeks, measured the muscle's size at rest before and after (MRI,
    ultrasound or biopsy), and either trained that exercise on its own or compared it with a control group or another
    exercise. Studies that only measured muscle activity, or muscle thickness during a contraction, or that trained
    several exercises together for the same muscle, are not enough on their own; nor are studies of patients (for
    example with back pain) applied to healthy adults.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Glutes</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). A 2025 systematic review of 12 training studies found that
      the gluteus maximus grew with back squats to parallel or deeper, the leg press, the hip thrust and progressive
      bodyweight leg exercises <Fragment set:html={cite("krause-neto-2025")} />. In a randomised trial, 9 weeks of hip
      thrusts or back squats (equal sets) grew the gluteus maximus similarly <Fragment set:html={cite("plotkin-2023")} />.
      The bodyweight study's programme was mostly lunges, Bulgarian split squats and skating squats; it thickened the
      gluteus maximus as much as barbell squats did <Fragment set:html={cite("wei-2023")} />, so the planner's bodyweight
      lunge is marked †, but its plain bodyweight squat is not.
      Squats to only 90° of knee bend grew it less, or not significantly, in the studies reviewed, so the planner's squat is
      to parallel or lower. No study isolated the deadlift, Romanian deadlift, kettlebell swing, dumbbell lunge, goblet
      squat or back extension, so those stay marked ◇. In the bodyweight study the rectus femoris (part of the
      quadriceps) didn't thicken significantly, so it gives no support for the quadriceps.</li>
    <li><strong>Hamstrings in hip thrusts.</strong> The hamstrings showed little to no growth with either the hip thrust
      or the back squat <Fragment set:html={cite("plotkin-2023")} />, so the planner no longer counts them for the hip
      thrust or the glute bridge (the same movement without a weight). For squats this matches Pelland and colleagues'
      table.</li>
    <li><strong>Hamstrings</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). In a randomised trial, 9 weeks of stiff-leg deadlifts
      (bending at the hips with the knees only slightly unlocked, as in the planner's Romanian deadlift) grew the
      hamstrings by about 7% more than in a control group, mostly the semimembranosus
      <Fragment set:html={cite("morin-2025")} />. In another, 10 weeks of 45° hip extensions (the planner's back extension)
      grew all three hamstring muscles, measured by MRI <Fragment set:html={cite("bourne-2017")} />. The leg curl is already
      in Pelland and colleagues' table. The conventional deadlift, single-leg Romanian deadlift and kettlebell swing weren't
      tested on their own, so their hamstrings stay marked ◇. Both studies' copies can't be redistributed, so the site
      owner keeps them privately (see the reference list).</li>
    <li><strong>Lats</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). No training study found has measured lat growth after one
      exercise on its own, so the lats stay marked ◇ in every exercise. The nearest: after 8 weeks of a programme whose
      only back exercises were lat pulldowns and seated rows, latissimus dorsi muscle fibres (from biopsies) grew by about
      22% <Fragment set:html={cite("paoli-2016")} />. That fits the planner's classification but can't separate the two
      exercises.</li>
    <li><strong>Calves</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). In a 12-week trial measured by MRI, standing calf raises grew
      the gastrocnemius by about 9–12% and the whole calf by about 6%, while seated calf raises (knees bent) barely grew
      the gastrocnemius <Fragment set:html={cite("kinoshita-2023")} />. The planner's calf raise is the standing version.
      The study used weights (70% of the most each person could lift once); a bodyweight calf raise may be easier than
      that. The leg curl's half set for the calves stays ◇.</li>
    <li><strong>Abs, lower back and forearms</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). No study meeting the standard above was
      found for the crunch, cable crunch, hanging knee raise, plank, back extension (for the lower back), floor back
      extension or farmer's carry. What exists measures muscle activity, muscle thickness during a contraction, programmes
      for people with back pain, or several exercises together. These classifications, including counting holds such as
      the plank and farmer's carry, stay marked ◇.</li>
    <li><strong>Chest and triceps in push-ups</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). In a randomised trial, 8 weeks of
      push-ups (adjusted, for example on the knees, to match a bench press at 40% of the most each person could lift once,
      done to failure) thickened the pectoralis major and triceps as much as bench pressing did
      <Fragment set:html={cite("kikuchi-2017")} />. The push-up's front-shoulder credit stays ◇ (not measured).</li>
    <li><strong>Side shoulders in lateral raises</strong> (checked 20 November 2026). In trained lifters, 8 weeks of
      dumbbell or cable lateral raises, the only side-deltoid exercise they were allowed, grew the side deltoid by 3.3–4.6%,
      with no difference between the two <Fragment set:html={cite("larsen-2025")} />. There was no control group. The side
      and rear shoulders' credits in presses and rows stay ◇.</li>
    <li><strong>Not supported yet.</strong> No study meeting the standard was found for the other ◇ credits of these
      muscles: chest in the dumbbell bench press, machine chest press and pec fly; triceps and front shoulders in dips and
      the pike push-up; front shoulders in dumbbell and machine shoulder presses; biceps, upper back and rear shoulders in
      pull-ups and rows; the trapezius in shrugs, face pulls and band pull-aparts; and the quadriceps and hamstrings in
      bodyweight and goblet squats, lunges, deadlifts, single-leg Romanian deadlifts and kettlebell swings. A study of
      one-arm dumbbell rows found some biceps growth near the shoulder only; it isn't open access and only its summary was
      read, so it isn't used.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Muscles that only hold the body still (such as the trunk muscles in a squat) are listed as "steadied" and not counted,
    except in exercises whose whole purpose is the hold, such as the plank or the farmer's carry. Counting those is also
    the planner's assumption: the analysis only included exercises that move through a range (lifting and lowering).
  </p>

  <details>
    <summary>All {strength.length} exercises and their muscles</summary>
    <div class="details-body table-scroll">
      <p class="small">◇ = the planner's own classification (an assumption); † = the planner's classification, supported by
        studies that measured muscle growth (<a href="#growth-evidence">which</a>). The last column gives the exercise name exactly as
        printed in the study's table, for each muscle taken from it.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Exercise</th><th>Needs</th><th>Direct (1 set)</th><th>Indirect (½ set)</th><th>From Pelland et al., Table 1A</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {strength.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.name}<br /><span class="muted small">{e.moves}</span></td>
              <td class="small">{needs(e.needs)}</td>
              <td>{cell(e, e.direct)}</td>
              <td>{cell(e, e.indirect)}</td>
              <td class="small">{sourced(e) || "None"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2 id="heart-and-lungs">Heart and lungs</h2>
  <p>
    Only continuous, rhythmic activities count towards heart and lungs: walking, running, cycling, rowing, the
    cross-trainer, skipping, stair climbing and swimming. The WHO defines aerobic activity as the body's large muscles
    moving rhythmically for a sustained period, and anaerobic activity as brief, intense bursts such as weightlifting and
    sprints <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2020")} />. Kettlebell swings and burpees have high MET values but are usually
    done in short bursts with rests, so the planner shows their values and doesn't count them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Activities are rated by their MET value (metabolic equivalent: how many times the energy used sitting at rest),
    copied unchanged, with activity codes, from the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities, which compiles measured
    energy costs for adults aged 19 to 59 and is meant for adults without a disability or a significant health condition
    affecting energy use <Fragment set:html={cite("compendium-2024")} />. The Compendium still contains 207 estimated values
    and marks them in red on its website; none of the values used here is one of them (checked 20 November 2026). People
    aged 60 and over have a separate Older Adult Compendium, with values on a different resting baseline, which this
    planner doesn't use <Fragment set:html={cite("compendium-2024")} />.
  </p>
  <p>
    The WHO defines moderate intensity as 3 to under 6 METs (usually 5 or 6 on a personal scale of 0 to 10) and vigorous
    intensity as 6 METs or more (usually 7 or 8). For adults aged 18 to 64 it recommends at least 150–300 minutes of
    moderate-intensity or at least 75–150 minutes of vigorous-intensity aerobic activity a week (or an equivalent mix),
    plus muscle-strengthening activities at moderate or greater intensity involving all major muscle groups on 2 or more
    days a week. For people aged 65 and over it adds varied multicomponent activity that emphasises functional balance
    and strength training, on 3 or more days a week <Fragment set:html={cite("who-2020")} />. The planner doesn't yet
    tailor its suggestions to age.
  </p>
  <p>
    The "Major muscle groups (WHO)" button chooses all {MUSCLES.length} muscle groups, without heart and lungs. The WHO names
    legs, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms as the major muscle groups; every group here belongs to one of those
    (for example, glutes, quadriceps, hamstrings and calves to legs; forearms to arms). That assignment is the planner's own
    reading of the WHO's list, and the WHO doesn't say how many sets count as involving a group.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>All {aerobic.length} activities with Compendium values</summary>
    <div class="details-body table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Code</th><th class="num">MET</th><th>WHO intensity</th><th>Counts for heart and lungs</th><th>Compendium description</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {aerobic.flatMap((e) => e.met!.map((r, i) => (
            <tr><td>{i === 0 ? e.name : ""}</td><td>{r.code}</td><td class="num">{r.met.toFixed(1)}</td><td>{band(r.met)}</td><td>{i === 0 ? (e.kind === "aerobic" ? "yes" : "no (short bursts)") : ""}</td><td class="small">{r.label}</td></tr>
          )))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Charts</h2>
  <p>
    The charts show fractional sets per muscle from one set of each exercise in a suggestion, on a linear scale that
    grows to fit the largest value and never cuts values off. The navy line marks the covered level for your target muscles,
    and every value is written next to its point, with ✓ or "short", so colour is never the only signal. With fewer than
    three target muscles a bar chart is used, as a radar chart needs at least three axes. Each chart has a "Show as table" option with the same numbers, for small screens and screen readers.
    Heart and lungs is not a muscle, so it is not on the charts; each suggestion states what covers it instead.
  </p>

  <h2 id="animations">"Show how" drawings</h2>
  <p>
    Some exercises have a small animation under them: a stick figure doing the movement, seen from the side and from the
    front at the same moment. Each is drawn by this site from the instructions on the exercise's "How to do it" page, and
    shown only for the version of the exercise it draws (for example, the dumbbell curl, not the barbell curl). The figure
    is built as one pose in three dimensions and drawn from two directions, so the two views always agree. Its
    proportions are round numbers chosen to look like an adult, not measurements. It has no hands, and its trunk bends
    only at the waist (enough for a crunch, not a rounded or arched back), so it can't show technique details such as a
    flat back or where the weight sits on the feet; the cues under
    each drawing, summarised from the linked page, carry those. Angles the page doesn't give (for example how far the
    trunk leans in a squat) are this site's choices, and are listed in <code>VERIFICATION.md</code> with what each drawing
    was compared with and when.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only one drawing moves at a time: the one most in view, or the last one you pressed Play on. If your device asks for
    reduced motion, none moves until you press Play. The drawings are plain pictures made in your browser; nothing is
    loaded from other sites.
  </p>
  <Caveat kind="illustration">The drawings have not been reviewed by a fitness professional. They show the general
    movement only and may be wrong in detail: follow the instructions on the linked page before trying an exercise.</Caveat>

  <h2>What has been checked</h2>
  <p>
    Checked line by line against the archived copies: every MET value, code and description; the fractional counting
    method and every classification taken from Table 1A; the WHO recommendations and intensity definitions; and the
    muscle actions used from the anatomy textbook. Details, with dates, are in the repository's
    <code>tools/exercise-planner/VERIFICATION.md</code>.
  </p>
  <Caveat kind="unverified">Every one of the planner's own classifications has been compared with studies that
    measured muscle growth (<a href="#growth-evidence">above</a>, searched 20 November 2026). Those still marked ◇ have
    no such study yet and rest on anatomy alone; they will be rechecked as new studies appear.</Caveat>

  <h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>
  <ul class="prose">
    <li>The suggestions are lists of exercises, not a training programme: they don't say how heavy, how many repetitions,
      how many sets or how often. Do any new exercise with good technique, starting light.</li>
    <li>"Quadriceps" combines four muscles. The study's table classifies squats, lunges and the leg press as direct for
      three of them but only indirect for the rectus femoris <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />; the planner
      counts them as direct for the group.</li>
    <li>{MUSCLES.length} muscle groups is a simplification. Within a group, different exercises stress different parts (for
      example, the upper and lower chest).</li>
    <li>Direct and indirect are two broad categories. Real exercises sit on a continuum, and technique, grip and range of
      motion change which muscles work hardest.</li>
    <li>The analysis behind the counting method pooled studies lasting about 10 weeks on average, in healthy participants
      with an average age of about 25 and none over 70, training with lifting and lowering movements
      <Fragment set:html={cite("pelland-2026")} />. It may apply less well to older people, very experienced lifters, or
      holds such as the plank.</li>
    <li>The studies controlled how heavy each set was and how close to failure it went. A set of a bodyweight exercise,
      such as a bodyweight squat, may be much easier than that for a trained person, so counting it as a full set can
      overstate the work.</li>
    <li>MET values are averages for adults aged 19 to 59; your own effort depends on your fitness and pace
      <Fragment set:html={cite("compendium-2024")} />.</li>
    <li>Muscles that only hold the body still aren't counted, although they are under tension and some authors suggest
      counting them as part of a set <Fragment set:html={cite("schoenfeld-2019")} />. The charts may therefore understate
      the work for, say, the lower back in a deadlift.</li>
    <li>"Covered" means "trained by the list at least once", the planner's own threshold for building lists. It is not a
      recommended dose.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <ul class="prose small">
